  "parentCard": {
    "title": "The ten-stones walk",
    "minutes": 15,
    "includes": "why-walk",
    "steps": [
      "Head outside with a small bag and hunt for stones together, one at a time, counting each one out loud as it goes in: one, two, three.",
      "Stop at ten. Tip them out in a line, count the line together slowly, then push them into one pile and say it: that is a ten.",
      "Keep hunting, but leave the ten pile alone. Put each new stone down beside it and count on out loud together: ten and one, ten and two, ten and three.",
      "On the way home let them pick something you passed and wonder about it together, like why are some stones smooth and some sharp? No right answer needed."
    ],
    "notice": [
      "Do they leave the ten pile alone and count on, or start again from one every time a new stone lands?",
      "Which comes out more easily, eleven, or ten and one?",
      "Did they ask a why question of their own on the walk?"
    ]
  }
